.1,2011,53,137,32],[18108,16.4,2011,53,131,33],[18405,15.0,2012,54,150,32],[18406,15.1,2012,49,141,31],[18407,15.4,2011,51,129,28],[18408,15.8,1956,46,119,31],[18707,15.8,1956,51,111,29],[18708,16.4,1956,45,103,31]],"links":[["Quay County in the Farmland Atlas","/farmland-atlas/new-mexico/quay-county"],["ARC or PLC in Quay County","/arc-plc/new-mexico/quay-county"],["PRF calculator","/prf"]],"story":"<p class=\"prf-lede prf-story\">As of Oct 12, Quay County&rsquo;s record runs 75 years back to 1951, and on the middle value of the county&rsquo;s 20 grids November to December came in under 90% of normal in 43 of them. The open year has been short so far, with February to March at 1% of normal in 2026 and March to April at 9% in 2026 on the middle of the grids. July to August is the wettest pair at 5.58 inches of normal, and January to February the driest at 0.81. Grid 17504&rsquo;s driest year was 1956 at 42% of normal.</p>"}
